items.content:
The Promise `.then/.catch` chain here would read more clearly as an `async` function with `try/catch`, which is the project-preferred style and avoids the nested `if/else` branching over the untyped response. Example:

```tsx
const handleFix = async (name: string) => {
  push("已发起重新捕获 · " + name + " · 请在弹出的指纹浏览器私信页完成验证");
  api.addLog("INFO", `启动自检·重新捕获 · ${name}`).catch(() => {});
  try {
    const r = await api.autoRecapture(name);
    if (r?.ok) push(r.msg || "已弹出指纹浏览器（私信页）");
    else push("重新捕获失败: " + (r?.msg || "未知错误"));
  } catch (e: unknown) {
    push("重新捕获异常: " + String(e));
  }
};
```
Note that `api.autoRecapture` is typed as `Promise<{ ok: boolean; msg: string }>`, so casting it to `{ ok?: boolean; msg?: string; error?: string }` and reading `error` relies on a field the API contract never declares.

